<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julia Domna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 160–<a href="/years/217">217</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julia Domna was a Roman empress, daughter of the high priest of the sun god at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and was the mother of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> and Geta, and she accompanied Severus on his campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against the Caledonians. Her family, including her great-nephews <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a>, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julia-domna.jpg" alt="Julia Domna" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julia_Domna_(Julia_Pia),_inv._2210,_Roman_-_Braccio_Nuovo,_Museo_Chiaramonti_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC00897.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julia Domna was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, modern Homs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, around 160, into a wealthy Arab family that had provided priest-kings of the temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> and had entered the senatorial aristocracy. She was the youngest daughter of the high priest Julius Bassianus and the sister of Julia Maesa. Her name Domna is said to be an archaic Arabic word meaning black, referring to the black stone in which the god appeared.</p><p>After losing his first wife about 186, the politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> sought her as his wife; the Historia Augusta says he had heard a prophecy of a woman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who would marry a king. They married in the summer of 187 at Lugdunum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>), where he was governor. The marriage is described as a happy one, and she won a reputation for letters and philosophy. Their sons were Lucius Septimius Bassianus, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>, born in 188, and Publius Septimius Geta, born the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> was murdered in 192, Pertinax and Didius Julianus held the throne briefly before Severus claimed it in 193. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pescennius-niger">Pescennius Niger</a> at Issus in 194 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodius-albinus">Clodius Albinus</a> at Lugdunum in 197, and Domna became empress. Unusually for an imperial wife, she accompanied her husband on campaign and stayed in camp with the army. She received many titles, among them Mother of the Invincible Camps and Mother of Augustus, and appeared on coins as Julia Augusta.</p><p>When Severus died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> (York) in 211, his sons were to rule jointly, and Domna acted as mediator between them. In December Caracalla's soldiers murdered Geta, and his name was removed from inscriptions. She received the title Pia Felix Augusta, perhaps implying that she continued her husband's attributes. She accompanied Caracalla to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> and later lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 217 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> began a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, and Domna stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to help with his correspondence. When he was assassinated by a soldier she killed herself, perhaps hastened by breast cancer and by reluctance to return to private life. Her sister Julia Maesa restored the family's rule in 218, and her nieces Julia Soaemias and Julia Mamaea were the mothers of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a>.</p><p>Her body was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and was later moved with Geta's remains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Mausoleum of Hadrian</a>. She is thought to have encouraged Philostratus to write the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-tyana">Apollonius of Tyana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julia Domna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cassius Dio later portrays the Caledonians and Maeatae as the two main groupings beyond the walls, and tells of the Caledonian Argentocoxus, whose wife bantered with the empress <b>Julia Domna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/caledonii">Caledonii</a></li><li><blockquote>The priestly family survived, and <b>Julia Domna</b> married Septimius Severus; her great-nephew Elagabalus became emperor in 218 CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/emesene-dynasty">Emesene dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.73,36.72&amp;year=197" aria-label="Homs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5482 907 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julia Domna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5932 1177h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5932 1177h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.73,36.72&amp;year=197">What was here in 197 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
